The verdict verbs' pre-run discipline: exit 2 is "the question could not be
asked", and a failure **before** the question — a session that will not open, a
context that resolves to nothing, a ledger with no registry to walk — must land
there, never on 1. Their 1 is a *verdict*: `check cutover`'s 1 says "the old
family still speaks", and reaching it through main's anyhow edge on a dead
router told CI exactly that about a bus nobody listened to.

`exit::asked` is the seam, and `zenctl/src/exit.rs` §"Where each code comes
from" is the whole doctrine in one place.

The fixture is the same dead client-mode bus as `session-transport-fallback`:
`connect.timeout_ms: 0`, `exit_on_failure: true`, so the failure is immediate
and without retry.

```console
$ zenctl check cutover --old-root 'old/**' --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!
check cutover: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

`check expect` reserves the same 2 ("session failure" is in its help text),
and its session open honours it too — not just the observation window.

```console
$ zenctl check expect 'v1/**' --for 1 --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!
check expect: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

A context that names nothing is a 2 everywhere now (`session-config-error`
pins the listing half), and here it is a 2 for a second, older reason as well:
`why`'s 1 is the claim "a cause was established", and a config file that does
not contain the name you gave establishes nothing.

```console
$ zenctl why 'v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/state/sysinfo/health' --context no-such-context
? 2
Error: context "no-such-context" not found in [..]config.toml — `zenctl context list`
why: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

`check retired` walks a ledger; no `--registry` means there is no ledger to
walk — unproven, not "still speaks" and not a pass.

```console
$ zenctl check retired
? 2
Error: check retired walks the [[deprecated]] ledger of local registry files — pass --registry <dir> (or set one on the active context)
check retired: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

`check schema --producer` already had the vocabulary (`not checked:`) and
routes its session failure through it — a dead bus is silence about the type,
not a claim about the payload.

```console
$ zenctl check schema --type Health --from '{}' --producer sysinfo --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
check schema: not checked: no session, so sysinfo's served describe is out of reach: failed to open the Zenoh session: Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!
check schema: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

So is the invocation that names no schema source at all — a check that never
happened, which used to come back through `?` as exit 1, this verb's "does not
conform".

```console
$ zenctl check schema --type Health --from '{}'
? 2
check schema: not checked: give --producer (live describe) or --schema-set FILE — the registry TOMLs carry type names, not shapes (RFC 08 §7)
check schema: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

## `why`'s polarity flipped (#307)

`why` used to exit **0** when it found a cause and **1** when the fleet was
healthy — which pages you for a healthy fleet and stays quiet when something
is wrong. Under the one contract, 1 is the *finding*, and a cause is exactly
that. Nothing in `cmd/why.rs` spells the flip: `WhyVerdict::to_judgement`
carries it (RFC 13's convention — the judged claim is the finding) and
`zenkey_fleet::judgement_exit_code` projects it, so the two frontends cannot
drift apart on what a `why` verdict means.
